---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout
  title="Halftone — Varve Docs"
  description="Create, edit, and export non-destructive halftone and process-screening looks in Varve."
>
  <div class="container-custom">
    <article class="doc-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Docs</a> / <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/color')}>Color &amp; Effects</a> / Halftone
      </nav>
      <h1>Halftone</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A non-destructive adjustment that screens any scoped content &mdash; images, vector shapes,
        text, groups &mdash; into clustered dots, lines, or stochastic texture, and keeps the editable
        source underneath.
      </p>

      <h2>Apply a halftone</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Select the artwork (or leave nothing selected to affect the page) and create an
          <strong>Adjustment Layer</strong>.</li>
        <li>In the Adjustments tab choose <strong>Add adjustment &rarr; Halftone</strong>.</li>
        <li>Pick a <strong>Preset</strong> as a starting point, then tune frequency, angle, and
          shape. The source stays editable; toggling the adjustment off restores it exactly.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Read the controls</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Method</strong> &mdash; AM uses clustered dots (classic print look); FM uses a
          stochastic threshold on a single density plane (CMYK FM follows the black separation).
          FM offers <strong>Blue noise</strong> (recommended, preview and export match),
          <strong>Bayer</strong> (regular ordered pattern), and <strong>Error diffusion</strong>
          (full-frame export quality).</li>
        <li><strong>Shape</strong> &mdash; round, elliptical, square, diamond, line, cross, or ring.
          Shape selection changes the dot geometry without changing tone.</li>
        <li><strong>Frequency (LPI)</strong> &mdash; lines per inch in document units. The panel
          shows the resulting period in pixels at 96&nbsp;ppi; when the period drops below about
          3&nbsp;px, raise the export resolution instead of trusting the preview.</li>
        <li><strong>Angle</strong> &mdash; mono screens only. Process (CMYK) screens use standard
          press angles per ink.</li>
        <li><strong>Threshold</strong> shifts how much of the tone becomes ink; <strong>Intensity</strong>
          blends the screened result with the original; <strong>Softness</strong> anti-aliases dot
          edges without changing coverage.</li>
        <li><strong>Ink / Paper</strong> colors are available for single-ink screens, and
          <strong>Alpha &rarr; Screen ink into alpha</strong> turns a mono screen into a true
          ink-on-transparency screentone.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Process screening (CMYK)</h2>
      <p>
        Selecting the <strong>CMYK</strong> channel screens each process ink separately at its own
        angle and recombines them for a preview. Advanced controls expose per-channel angles and
        registration offsets, <strong>black generation</strong> (GCR or shadow-only UCR) with
        strength, a <strong>total ink limit</strong>, and a <strong>Show ink</strong> selector for
        inspecting a single plate. This is an artistic process-screen preview, not an ICC
        separation or press-calibrated proof.
      </p>

      <h2>Document-space behavior</h2>
      <p>
        The screen is anchored in document coordinates: panning and zooming never shift the pattern
        or change its ruling, and two objects at the same document position share the same screen
        phase. Export at higher resolution resolves more samples per cell while keeping the same
        physical frequency.
      </p>

      <h2>Legacy screens</h2>
      <p>
        Documents created before the corrected 2026 screen contract keep their original rendering so
        artwork does not change underneath you. Their Inspector shows <strong>Legacy screen</strong>
        with an <strong>Update to corrected screening</strong> action that keeps every setting and
        renders the requested ruling and shape accurately. New effects always start on the corrected
        contract.
      </p>

      <h2>Export</h2>
      <p>
        Halftones export through the canonical render pipeline at the requested resolution. SVG and
        PDF cannot encode a screen structurally, so the affected subtree is rasterized with the full
        filter stack and embedded while the rest of the page stays vector. Preview and export use
        the same algorithm for AM and ordered FM; error diffusion applies only to full-frame export.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <p>
        Screening is CPU-based. CMYK and duotone modes are uncalibrated artistic simulations, not
        press-ready separations, and standard screen angles are conventions rather than a universal
        moiré guarantee. Halftone output is raster, not editable vector dots; there is no vector
        conversion export.
      </p>

      <p><a href={sitePath('/features/color-effects')}>Read the color and effects overview →</a></p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .doc-page { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 3rem 0 5rem; }
  .breadcrumb { font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  /* Display typography: the bespoke hero ramp is page art, not a shared role. */
  h1 { font-family: var(--font-editorial); font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 2.75rem); color: var(--text-primary); }
  .lead { font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text-secondary); margin-bottom: 2.5rem; }
  h2 { margin-top: 2.5rem; color: var(--text-primary); }
  p, li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.75; }
  li + li { margin-top: 0.65rem; }
  code { color: var(--text-primary); }
</style>
